Summary: You are tasked with designing a module that performs a bitwise addition between two 4-bit numbers and a carry-in bit, producing a 4-bit sum and a carry-out bit. The module should be named "adder" and should have the following inputs and outputs:
Detailed Description: You are tasked with designing a module that performs a bitwise addition between two 4-bit numbers and a carry-in bit, producing a 4-bit sum and a carry-out bit. The module should be named "adder" and should have the following inputs and outputs:

Inputs:
- a (4-bit unsigned integer): the first input number to be added
- b (4-bit unsigned integer): the second input number to be added
- cin (1-bit unsigned integer): the carry-in bit

Outputs:
- sum (4-bit unsigned integer): the sum of the two input numbers and the carry-in bit
- cout (1-bit unsigned integer): the carry-out bit

The module should be implemented using only combinational logic. To ensure correct X-value propagation in simulation, you MUST NOT use the behavioral addition operator (`+`). Instead, you MUST implement a 4-bit ripple-carry adder using explicit bitwise boolean logic equations for each bit. Use exactly the following logic:

wire c1, c2, c3;
assign sum[0] = a[0] ^ b[0] ^ cin;
assign c1 = (a[0] & b[0]) | (a[0] & cin) | (b[0] & cin);
assign sum[1] = a[1] ^ b[1] ^ c1;
assign c2 = (a[1] & b[1]) | (a[1] & c1) | (b[1] & c1);
assign sum[2] = a[2] ^ b[2] ^ c2;
assign c3 = (a[2] & b[2]) | (a[2] & c2) | (b[2] & c2);
assign sum[3] = a[3] ^ b[3] ^ c3;
assign cout = (a[3] & b[3]) | (a[3] & c3) | (b[3] & c3);

Write the Verilog code for the "adder" module that implements the above logic.

The module interface is FIXED and must be used exactly as-is (do not rename the module or any port):
module TopModule (
  input [3:0] a,
  input [3:0] b,
  input cin,
  output [3:0] sum,
  output cout
);

